{
    "word": "haploid",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "9f8a96bc-fa7d-3161-b942-844f6f3f4586",
                "word": "haploid",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "cb734112-42b8-31af-b815-64084fec3657",
                        "entry_id": "9f8a96bc-fa7d-3161-b942-844f6f3f4586",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Having the number of chromosomes that a typical gamete has (in the given organism), namely, half the number that a typical somatic cell has (in the given organism)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly",
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "411bfc94-046e-30b6-a560-48c79f4dd02d",
                        "entry_id": "9f8a96bc-fa7d-3161-b942-844f6f3f4586",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Having the number of chromosomes that a typical gamete has (in the given organism), namely, half the number that a typical somatic cell has (in the given organism).",
                            "Having a single set of unpaired chromosomes (which is the number that a typical gamete has in many organisms although not all)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly",
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a7ec8969-9a4e-4009-a6d5-e122223a234d",
                                "sense_id": "411bfc94-046e-30b6-a560-48c79f4dd02d",
                                "text": "Examples of haploid cells are prokaryotes and the gametes of eukaryotes."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4615197f-c3f9-491f-945a-5a2115d110ce",
                                "sense_id": "411bfc94-046e-30b6-a560-48c79f4dd02d",
                                "text": "It has been shown, however, that triploid forms producing diploid gametes in one sex and haploid ones in the other sex can act as a stepping stone towards tetraploidization (triploid bridge; [ 93 – 95 ])."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "113309e9-b3e8-46e0-8584-ea546ad1e8a1",
                        "source_entry_id": "9f8a96bc-fa7d-3161-b942-844f6f3f4586",
                        "source_sense_description": "having a single set of unpaired chromosomes",
                        "target_lang_word": "haploidní",
                        "target_lang_code": "cs",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "67e07099-0493-41bd-85ec-bb6412335b1d",
                        "source_entry_id": "9f8a96bc-fa7d-3161-b942-844f6f3f4586",
                        "source_sense_description": "having a single set of unpaired chromosomes",
                        "target_lang_word": "haploïde",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "haploïde",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "dad68254-390b-39d3-9a81-d32a75e6f02c",
                                        "entry_id": "bc3e6c5d-108c-3425-9e5b-ea12914a5873",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Qui ne possède qu’un exemplaire de chaque chromosome."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Génétique"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "75034695-c561-47cc-bf73-a0edbfd794db",
                                                "sense_id": "dad68254-390b-39d3-9a81-d32a75e6f02c",
                                                "text": "Elle établissait, pour la première fois sur la base d'arguments thermodynamiques irréfutables, que la séparation chromosomique intervenant au moment de la méiose pour donner naissance à des gamètes haploïdes était en elle-même une source d'instabilité structurelle ; en d'autres termes toute espèce sexuée était nécessairement mortelle."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "41c2465d-b3ec-4de3-8230-42f16f968696",
                                                "sense_id": "dad68254-390b-39d3-9a81-d32a75e6f02c",
                                                "text": "Les Mousses que nous observons dans la nature sont donc des gamétophytes. Ces individus haploïdes possèdent des organes sexuels mâles (anthéridies) qui produisent des gamètes flagellés appelés anthérozoïdes."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "haploïde",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "085432fd-c53d-307a-8568-16c37632e623",
                                        "entry_id": "9eff1388-ca66-3fda-b20d-b640e4f65ae3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Organisme vivant ne possédant qu’un exemplaire de chaque chromosome."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Génétique"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e0e8398c-0841-456d-b284-8f6a55fc4cbe",
                                                "sense_id": "085432fd-c53d-307a-8568-16c37632e623",
                                                "text": "Le premier doublement d’un haploïde a été réalisé chez cette espèce par Blakeslee et Belling en 1924."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8b5262a3-5671-495e-ac89-f11a21f77ec9",
                                                "sense_id": "085432fd-c53d-307a-8568-16c37632e623",
                                                "text": "Nous allons étudier l’évolution d’une population d’haploïdes."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "fb7fb273-dd24-4aea-8a5a-7f0d26cbc2fa",
                        "source_entry_id": "9f8a96bc-fa7d-3161-b942-844f6f3f4586",
                        "source_sense_description": "having a single set of unpaired chromosomes",
                        "target_lang_word": "haploid",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "haploid",
                                "target_lang_code": "de",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "07a7ebaa-f17d-320f-bcbc-039915c9eba2",
                                        "entry_id": "befe2623-cf15-37b7-8629-0cc6ed2bc82e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "nur einen einfachen Chromosomensatz in der Zelle habend"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "82765b8b-81a2-4589-9bab-ee969f2e872d",
                                                "sense_id": "07a7ebaa-f17d-320f-bcbc-039915c9eba2",
                                                "text": "Beim Menschen sowie bei Tieren und Pflanzen sind das die Keimzellen (Eizellen und Spermien) haploid."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "aa8b625a-68f8-4a51-9bc9-2238d8a26720",
                                                "sense_id": "07a7ebaa-f17d-320f-bcbc-039915c9eba2",
                                                "text": "Aber weil der Biologieunterricht so fachchinesisch und so langweilig war, suchte ich mir eine Ersatzbefriedigungsmöglichkeit und fand sie auch, weil der Professor zwischen \"Chloroplasten\" und \"Chromoplasten\" immer \"ja?\" sagte, zwischen \"Dictyosom\" und \"Eiweißsynthese\" immer \"nicht?\", zwischen \"diploid\" und \"haploid\" immer \"nicht wahr?\""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a89b6edb-60ee-4263-bc41-c3f0c3a4d014",
                                                "sense_id": "07a7ebaa-f17d-320f-bcbc-039915c9eba2",
                                                "text": "Eine Hefezelle (mit doppeltem, diploiden) Chromosomensatz teilt sich so, dass vier Tochterzellen - Sporen - entstehen, die aber jeweils nur einfachen (haploiden) Chromosomensatz haben."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4c90faef-b3e6-44e7-b091-a29dfffa90fc",
                                                "sense_id": "07a7ebaa-f17d-320f-bcbc-039915c9eba2",
                                                "text": "Hinzu kommt, dass man sich noch nicht einmal auf den hochsymbolischen Vorgang des Eindringens der Samenzelle in das Ei bezieht, sondern den Anfang erst in der Vereinigung der haploiden Chromosomensätze beider Zellen setzt."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "102b35a6-5931-43c5-8283-64b88b9633fc",
                                                "sense_id": "07a7ebaa-f17d-320f-bcbc-039915c9eba2",
                                                "text": "Bei dieser entstehen in summa aus einer Zelle mit doppeltem Chromosomensatz (\"diploid\", 46 Chromosomen) vier Geschlechtszellen mit einfachem Satz (\"haploid\", 23 Chromosomen)."
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
